<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Storming of the Bastille</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The storming of the Bastille was the capture of the Bastille, a royal fortress and prison in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, by armed crowds on 14 July 1789. Fearing a royal move against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, Parisians seized weapons and attacked the fortress in search of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, and its governor, the Marquis de Launay, was killed. The event became the symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, and 14 July is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s national day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/storming-of-the-bastille.jpg" alt="Storming of the Bastille" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
